3.2 Engineering the Reverse Infrastructure

• Induced Memory Amnesia: The local Ring 0 driver executes low-level kernel calls (MmAllocateContiguousMemory) to seize a user-defined quota (e.g., 10GB out of 16GB). It alters the CPU page translation tables, forcing the Windows OS to believe the system physically only possesses 6GB of RAM. The hijacked 10GB is entirely hidden from the local OS scheduler.
• The Remote NUMA (rNUMA) Bridge: The driver maps this locked memory block to an active kernel network stack. Using an internet-optimized implementation of the RoCEv2 (RDMA over Converged Ethernet) protocol encapsulated within error-corrected UDP architectures (such as Google's QUIC), it bridges the hardware to the cloud cluster gateway.
• Decentralized Model Partitioning: The remote AI cluster registers the client's memory as a native physical slot expansion. Tensor matrices are loaded directly into the client's RAM via Direct Memory Access (DMA), completely bypassing the user's local operating system or user-space applications.

